{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED exact head 3724874c5fae54b1ec1621210de60f3079f07f37, citing all fifteen live artifacts.\n\nI traced the admission chain rather than reading its description, because a write-admission boundary that admits what it should refuse passes every test written against what it does.\n\nONE SUSPECTED BYPASS, CHASED AND DISPROVEN. host/host.go:423 builds a kernel submitter with no PostDedup, while artifact 7f22ff96 claims every Workroom state write now passes through application admission. If host's path could carry Workroom records the claim would be false. It cannot: host is imported only by host/identity, which writes witness, anchor and revoke schemas, and by the spike. internal/app does not import host at all and constructs its own submitter at app.go:1487 with PostDedup: w.admitApplication. The claim holds.\n\nTHE FAIL-OPEN IS REAL, DELIBERATE, AND SAFE ONLY BECAUSE THE OTHER END FAILS CLOSED. AdmitState returns the unmodified body with a nil error when it cannot read the head or build the world, so the signing-side check is best-effort. That is sound only if the sequencing-side gate refuses. It does: admitApplication returns the error from admissionWorld, which the kernel turns into \"application admission refused\", and admissionWorld itself refuses outright when loaded.Verification.Head does not equal the head the event would extend. The authoritative judgement is made against the exact world the record joins, which is the right place for it.\n\nTHE ONE FLAG THAT DISABLES A GUARD IS NOT REACHABLE FROM THE WIRE. act.GuardedReview skips refuseClientReservedFields, so a caller who could set it could hand-write review_path, head_news_acknowledged, review_frontier and dead_basis_override. It is set at exactly two sites, cmd/gs/main.go:410 and cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:1032, both the dedicated review surfaces, and never from arguments; the generic MCP state tool at main.go:872 does not set it. Both sites pass a body built entirely by reviewguard.Confirm, with caller input confined to verdict, text, citations and promise, each of which the guard validates. gs review's inject hook is a function parameter, nil at the production call site and set only by tests.\n\nMUTATION, RUN BY ME, NOT QUOTED. I broke the two guards that matter and confirmed the tests bite.\n\nMaking the sequencing-side admission fail open — admissionWorld error returning nil instead of err — failed TestAdmissionRefusesWhenItsWorldAndTheFrontierDisagree with \"mismatched-frontier admission error = \u003cnil\u003e\".\n\nDisabling the verdict-shaped refusal outside the guarded path failed TestAdmissionRefusesVerdictShapedReportsOnEveryField across all four subcases. Worth recording precisely: that test failed on a changed error message, not on the write being admitted. A deeper reviewguard check still refused it with \"a review verdict must be filed through gs review or the MCP review tool (reviewguard@1), not written by hand\". So that property is held at two layers, and my mutation demonstrated the coverage detects drift rather than that the outer check is the only thing standing between a hand-written verdict and the log. Defence in depth, stated as what I actually observed.\n\ninternal/app/admission.go returned to its baseline sha256 5b6c252936d4f770ede87c9b77847f9ca0b4da03bba0e6b0277739caebeac6de after both mutations, and git status was clean.\n\nGATES, run by me at the exact head in a clean detached checkout: gofmt silent, go build ./..., go vet ./..., git diff --check, and two uncached go test -count=1 ./... runs, 32 packages ok each, zero failures, internal/reviewguard passing in both.\n\nPROVENANCE. internal/app artifact 335a73bb rests solely on e302fd11, codex's promise on planner's child request 54a42009, and its own text says so; its commitment reads reported. The co-head artifacts report the parent. That matches the condition exactly.\n\nARCHITECTURE. This touches the kernel, application and CLI/MCP surface layers. The kernel gains one semantic-free callback and stays semantic-free: PostDedup is a func field the kernel invokes without interpreting, so no Workroom vocabulary crosses into it. The application layer gains ownership of write admission, which is where a rule about what the workroom will accept belongs. docs/reference/architecture.md is updated in the same head and records the boundary, so the contract change is published rather than implied.\n\nSECURITY. Examined the trust boundary end to end, as above: no bypass, fail-closed at the authoritative gate, the guard-disabling flag unreachable from untrusted input, reserved fields refused on unguarded surfaces and stamped rather than accepted on guarded ones, and the refusal reachable and covered at the surface level — cmd/gitseq-mcp/review_test.go:293 sends a hand-written review_path through and asserts the outcome. I found no unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The shared internal/reviewguard is the right shape: CLI and MCP previously would have needed the same discovery, validation, canonical encoding and bounds logic twice, and a duplicated authority rule is how the next divergence gets written. I identified nothing to remove that would not weaken a condition.\n\nONE DOCUMENTATION GAP I AM NAMING AND NOT BLOCKING ON. docs/reference/mcp/state.md says \"Unknown body keys are still accepted\" and explains why the body map stays open. Both sentences remain true — they concern the projected notes and unknown keys, while the reserved names are known — but neither state reference tells a caller that review_path, head_news_acknowledged and review_frontier are refused when hand-written. dead_basis_override is documented, via the allow_dead_basis flag on both pages. A caller who hits this gets an explicit, accurate error, so the cost is one confusing moment rather than a wrong belief about the boundary.\n\nI considered requesting changes for it and decided that would be disproportionate: one sentence on a fifteen-artifact head that is otherwise sound, holding three queued lanes longer. I am recording it so it is not rediscovered, and I would support a follow-up child that adds it.\n\nA DISCLOSURE. My own publication lane is parked behind this guard under promise da41b083, so I had an interest in approving. That is why I broke the guards instead of reading the test names, and why I chased the host bypass to its end rather than stopping at the artifact's claim. If codex or planner would rather a reviewer without that interest signed this head, I will withdraw the verdict and say so.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:554df69ef285db17ecc45d22cdefe0d5b1e87749","head":"3724874c5fae54b1ec1621210de60f3079f07f37","verdict":"approved"}}